  "storage": "Keep tightly closed in a cool, dry place at room temperature, away from the stove's steam. Always use a completely dry spoon. Do not refrigerate (condensation makes it react). An opened sachet should be used at once or folded shut and used within a few days.",
  "looksLike": "A fine white powder that fizzes strongly the moment it touches water. Sold in small paper sachets (5 g) and white plastic bottles (100 g, 200 g); the plain one is labelled \"Regular\".",
  "howToPick": "Buy plain/\"Regular\" for cooking. Check the expiry date and that the bottle or sachet is sealed and dry. A quick test: a pinch in a little water should fizz hard at once; if it barely fizzes, it is stale and the dhokla will turn out flat (Veg Recipes of India, dish file sources).",
  "shelfLife": "Use by the printed expiry date. Once a bottle is opened, moisture slowly kills its fizz, so sachets are better if you use it rarely.",
